while staying with our coverage from Lib  Dem conference we're joined now by the  party's Foreign Affairs spokesman chicka  Runa thank you for being on the program  with us this morning  so are the Lib Dems on favor of revoking  article 50 and canceling practice  altogether well so long as this  Parliament persists Sophie of course  we're going to carry on campaigning for  a people's vote with remain on the  ballot which we will be of course  backing but that's an issue for this  Parliament if we enter a general  election which i think is probably quite  likely in November we will continue  providing that clear distinctive voice  as the biggest and strongest remain  party in this country by committing if  we have a Liberal Democrat majority  government to reverting revoking article  50 we believe that a victory in an  election like that would give us a clear  democratic mandate to do that and it  would thankfully bring an end to the  chaos that we have seen swiftly so we  can actually focus on the big issues  this country is facing how do we  properly fund our NHS how do we build a  fairer more equal society and transform  our dysfunctional economy you see you  say that you'd have a mandate for it but  at the same time I can't help but wonder  if it is really credible that a party  with Democrats in the actual title of  the party would be willing to overturn  the biggest Democratic vote we've  actually seen in British history without  even putting the question specifically  about the people well look we actually  want to see Parliament functioning so  that's why you have seen this party  arguing against the shutdown and  probation of Parliament that's what it  is to be democratic and we want to see  things determined at the ballot box  through votes and that's what you would  do in a general election I know it suits  people on the other side of the argument  particularly the extreme Bret suitors  who I think actually represent a  minority in our country to somehow say  that we had one vote in 2016 and somehow  democracy stopped at that point in any  further votes or mandates that we have  after that point don't count well that's  just not the way British democracy works  I could run with your argument if it was  a case of us saying no in no way shape  or form should we reference  it's back to the people but generally  the people in this country have their  say on the big issue through general  elections or referendums we're talking  about general election because of course  your party also voted against having an  early election when Boris Johnson put  that department when do you actually  want to see one happen once we have  secured the extension to article 50  which is now provided through the been  act of Parliament and once that comes  into effect and we can be absolutely  sure that there is no risk that in  holding a general election this country  falls off a cliff then fine let's go  ahead and have that general election and  see if we can resolve some of the mess  we've seen created by the big two  parties in British politics look I think  something fundamentally is going on  right here Sophie I was campaigning in  the cities of London and Westminster  with our team there I'm our candidate  there in the general election and I was  going to areas which it's unheard of to  think would vote anything other than  conservative where we see a collapse in  the conservative vote and that's because  we are seeing right now a  reconfiguration of British politics it's  interesting that you are going to be  fighting the seat over cities of London  and Westminster currently held of course  by the Conservatives and when I last  interviewed you on the show we did it in  Stratham where you were formerly an MP  and you said this is the community I'm  from and I grew up in this is my  community my family is here so why would  I leave it why would you leave it when  no one has an indefinite right to  represent a constituency in perpetuity  and I'm of course sad to be leaving  Stretton and to be clear this was my  decision we actually had a fantastic  candidate in Helen Thompson in Stratham  who'd already led a very strong European  election campaign earlier this year in  the constituency which saw the Liberal  Democrats win comfortably taking seven  of the eight wards and I didn't think it  was right to elbow her out of the  situation very strong candidate and also  be frank with you Sophie I wasn't  particularly relishing the prospect of  coming up against what I knew in the new  Labour Party not the old Labour Party  that selected me  ten years ago but the New Labour Party  that I'd be facing a pretty vile awful  an appalling campaign and I feel I owe a  duty of care to my staff and my family I  didn't really want to put them through  that but also we see of course the  cities of London and Westminster isn't  just a symbol nationally it is a global  symbol of open international liberal  values and it is currently represented  by a member of parliament who failed to  vote to block No Deal and who represents  a party which has basically become a  right-wing populist nationalist party I  mean what you saw with the ejection of  21 conservative MPs including Sam keema  who we are incredibly happy to now have  in the Liberal Democrats wasn't just an  injection of you know heavyweight  substantial individuals who command  respect across the House of Commons you  actually saw the objection of an entire  tradition the One Nation Disraeli  tradition from the Conservative Party  that was a message saying to them  there's no longer a home for you a party  like that cannot possibly adequately  represent an international global  community like that we find in the  cities of London and Westminster you  mentioned some jira there he of course  has not become the eighteenth Lib Dem MP  you've seen defections from the Labour  Party people such as yourselves and also  from the Conservative Party is there a  risk that the only thing that unites you  is your desires try and stop braixen no  look it's much more than that and I  think people you know misunderstanding  what's going on here  yes remain leave is indicative of  whether you want the UK to continue to  be a member of the European Union but  it's about much more than that it's  indicative of holding they sat a set of  liberal international open values where  you believe in multilateral cooperation  countries working together to improve  the lots of their people you believe in  the rule of your you believe in  upholding human rights that is the new  divide now I get you know between  frankly socially liberal  internationalists and on the other hand  authoritarian social conservatives which  you see increasingly the Tory party  representing in this country so I  actually think British politics now  is less class-based than it once was and  it's actually less left-right it's  actually more about whether you're  liberal authoritarian and the  authoritarian part of the you know  politics here is represented by the  Conservative Party and the clue is in  the name of the Liberal Democrats we are  the biggest and strongest remain party  but it's really about championing and  defending liberal values well let's talk  about liberal values shall we because  one of the defectors was Phillip Lee the  former Conservative MP in 2017 he  abstained on a vote on equal marriage  the year after he proposed that  immigrants come in to settle in Britain  she'll have to first prove they don't  have HIV is that someone you're  comfortable with being in same party  well you'll have to ask Phillip about  that all I know you know he's a former  GP very well respected across the House  of Commons his heart beats in a liberal  direction and I know that he's meeting  some of those who you know had concerns  about things that he said or done in the  past here at our conference but one of  the reasons that he has been arguing for  us to stay in the European unit is  because he thinks free movement and  immigration is a good thing so he can  speak for himself but I am confident  that he has our liberal values that and  as those that have been in this party  will tell you a lot longer than me if  fundamentally after a discussion and  we're having these kinds of discussions  with people it's clear that somebody  doesn't have our values they won't be in  this party but he clearly does and  that's why he is a Liberal Democrat  member of parliament so do you think  that abstaining on about on equal  marriages demonstrates the liberal  values well he supports equal marriage  and that's what matters right now I mean  look you can go and look into what  people have said and done in the past  I've been pretty critical of what my  party has done in the past in coalition  government of course Phillip was part of  that coalition government but I'm  confident looking at what has happened  particularly under Joseph Ince's  leadership that things change and this  party has changed which is unchanged for  the better reference to the same values  but that's why people are flocking to us  not just members of parliament we've got  over 120,000 members now there is an  energy and enthusiasm and optimism  positivity here which I have to say I  haven't experienced elsewhere  and just a quick final thought because  we are a little bit pressed for time now  and if there is an election we think  will be sooner rather than later with  the Lib Dems be prepared to go into  coalition again look we want to change  the voting system we think this  first-past-the-post voting system is  broken and we need a system and  proportional representation and with  proportional representation that will  need two more coalition governments so  we don't have any problem with the  concept of a coalition government which  you see across the continent in  different countries but lets us be clear  the two parties as they are currently  configured with their current leadership  are not parties but frankly we could see  ourselves doing business with right now

